# A school day from getting up to going to bed, each thing with its time on a clock.
lesson language-my-day v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=6 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "My day"
  goal "Say what you do at each time of the day in [[language.name]], from getting up to going to bed."

  look {
    scene 27x9 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row day space=2 align=end {
          clock morning h=7 m=0
          clock lunch h=2 m=0
          clock night h=10 m=0
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      Leo's day: [[day.i-get-up-at-seven]], I get up at seven. [[day.i-have-lunch-at-two]], I have
      lunch at two. [[day.i-go-to-bed-at-ten]], I go to bed at ten. In Spain lunch is often at two
      and dinner at nine. To ask about someone's day, say [[phrase.what-time-do-you-get-up]], what
      time do you get up?
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.my-day-name k=4
    }
    show language.my-day-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.my-day-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.my-day-write k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show language.my-day-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.my-day-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.my-day-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=4
    show language.my-day-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.my-day-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.my-day-write k=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.my-day-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.my-day-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[day.i-get-up-at-seven]] I get up at seven. [[phrase.what-time-do-you-get-up]] What time do you get up?"
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is the right time with the wrong thing done, or the reverse, and
    lunch and dinner taken for each other: each naming question shows the time on a clock and offers
    both mistakes beside the right sentence. That lunch in Spain is often at two and dinner at nine
    is recalled. The step up from the time of grade four and my week of this grade is the clock time
    inside a sentence about a routine, in order from morning to night. From this grade the written
    words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation
    is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is
    stuck, go through the child's own day with a toy clock, one sentence at each time. Off the page,
    keep a diary of one day in the language, a line for each time. It meets England's key stage 2
    languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "describe people, places, things and
    actions orally and in writing"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can talk about
    themselves after organizing the contents they want to communicate by using simple words and
    phrases and basic expressions" (Japan's grade four book Let's Try 2 has a unit on a day,
    recalled); and CEFR A2, "Can communicate in simple and routine tasks requiring a simple and
    direct exchange of information on familiar and routine matters". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[day.i-come-home-at-four]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[day.i-get-up-at-seven]] and [[day.i-have-breakfast-at-half-past-seven]] and the words to write are [[day.i-have-lunch]] and [[day.i-have-dinner]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[day.i-do-my-homework-at-five]] and [[day.i-have-dinner-at-nine]] and the words to write are [[day.i-come-back]], [[day.i-sleep]] and [[day.i-have-lunch]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
